# ThreadSanitizer reports a data race entirely inside OpenSSL's libcrypto
# when several concurrent client handshakes verify peer certificates against
# one shared trust store, which is what chttpclient's Tier 2/3 async engine
# does whenever it has more than one request in flight to a certificate-
# verifying HTTPS origin (tests.c's own
# async_tls.concurrent_https_requests_all_succeed).
#
# The report is always the same shape: ASN1_STRING_cmp reads a heap block
# that ASN1_STRING_set concurrently writes, the block allocated through
# CRYPTO_malloc, with every frame that touches it inside libcrypto.so.3.
# In spite of the report, every concurrent handshake completes and verifies
# correctly: what the detector reports is the access pattern, not a handshake
# failure.
#
# The race is libcrypto's own, not this project's. A standalone program that
# links nothing but libssl and libcrypto, and runs eight concurrent
# socketpair-backed client handshakes against one shared client SSL_CTX,
# reproduces the identical ASN1_STRING_cmp/ASN1_STRING_set report with no
# application frame in either racing access stack at all.
#
# Neither calling-side mitigation is usable, both measured against that same
# standalone program. Serialising each client's whole handshake call behind
# one mutex does remove the report; this module drives the handshake one
# non-blocking step at a time (ctls_conn_handshake_step, declared in
# include/internal/ctls.h), so that lock scope spans the network waits
# between steps and serialises concurrent connections' actual data transfer
# rather than a brief CPU-bound moment. Giving every connection its own
# SSL_CTX, and with it its own trust store, also removes this report, but
# exposes a different libcrypto-internal race in its place and costs a full
# trust-store rebuild per connection.
#
# The anchor names a libcrypto frame, never one of this project's own
# functions. A suppression matches on any frame of either racing access, so
# an anchor naming a function in this module silences a race in this
# library's own code just as readily as the third-party one it is written
# for; an anchor on the handshake-stepping call site silences a race whose
# only frames are that function itself. Two things keep that from happening
# quietly: the Makefile's check_tsan_supp target fails the build when an
# anchor below names a symbol this test binary defines, and test_tsan runs
# with print_suppressions=1 so every run prints how many times the anchor
# matched, leaving a dead suppression distinguishable from a working one.
race:ASN1_STRING_cmp
